# Pipistrel SW121 / SW128 — Flight Test Campaign

**Flight-test planning, execution, data reduction and performance / stability assessment on manned aircraft**

**Institution:** Politecnico di Milano  
**Course:** Flight Testing — MSc Aeronautical Engineering  
**Role:** Team Leader / Flight Test Engineer  
**Team:** 2 members  
**Academic Year:** 2024–2025  

---

## Project Overview

This project consisted of the planning, execution and post-processing of a structured flight-test campaign performed on two Pipistrel aircraft:

- **Pipistrel SW121 Explorer**
- **Pipistrel SW128 Velis Electro**

The objective was to verify and validate selected aircraft **performance, longitudinal dynamic stability and operational characteristics** through repeatable flight-test procedures, while comparing measured results with aircraft reference data and applicable certification criteria.

The two aircraft were deliberately flown using closely comparable mission profiles in order to support direct comparison between a conventional piston-powered aircraft and an electrically powered aircraft.

---

## My Role

I acted as **Team Leader and on-board Flight Test Engineer** within a two-person student team.

My responsibilities included:

- definition of flight-test objectives;
- test-point selection and sequencing;
- preparation of the complete flight-test plan;
- flight-zone and mission planning;
- preparation of flight cards;
- definition of measurement and data-acquisition requirements;
- preparation of pre-, in- and post-flight procedures;
- Test Hazard Analysis;
- coordination with the Flight Test Pilot;
- real-time test-point execution and data recording;
- post-flight data reduction;
- comparison with POH and certification-reference values;
- interpretation of anomalies and test limitations;
- preparation of technical conclusions and recommendations.

The campaign included **four flights** and approximately **100 directed test points** across the two aircraft.

---

## Test Aircraft

### Pipistrel SW121 Explorer

The SW121 Explorer is a two-seat, high-wing aircraft powered by a **Rotax 912 ULS piston engine** with a variable-pitch propeller.

The aircraft provided the conventional-propulsion reference for the campaign.

### Pipistrel SW128 Velis Electro

The SW128 Velis Electro is a two-seat, high-wing aircraft powered by a **57.6 kW electric propulsion system** with a fixed-pitch propeller.

Testing both aircraft under comparable procedures enabled assessment of differences in aircraft behaviour, propulsion characteristics and performance.

---

## Flight-Test Programme

The test programme covered four main areas.

### Take-off Performance

The campaign evaluated:

- ground roll;
- rotation speed;
- distance to the 50 ft obstacle;
- aircraft configuration;
- influence of mass, wind and atmospheric conditions;
- comparison with POH performance.

The procedure required coordinated call-outs and event marking between the Flight Test Pilot and Flight Test Engineer, while maintaining redundant manual and digital data acquisition.

---

### Longitudinal Dynamic Stability — Phugoid

Stick-fixed and stick-free phugoid manoeuvres were used to evaluate the aircraft's longitudinal dynamic response.

The analysis included:

- oscillation period;
- damping;
- airspeed evolution;
- altitude response;
- comparison between measured and predicted behaviour.

The flight-test results were also compared against a numerical aerodynamic model to assess the capability and limitations of simulation-based prediction.

---

### Stall Testing

Stall tests were performed in clean configuration using progressively different deceleration rates.

The assessment included:

- stall speed;
- stall warning;
- pitch-break behaviour;
- roll and yaw behaviour;
- control response;
- altitude loss;
- repeatability;
- compliance with applicable handling criteria.

Particular attention was given to identifying whether each test point could be considered valid for certification-style evidence.

---

### Landing Performance

Landing testing assessed:

- stabilised approach conditions;
- speed at the 50 ft reference height;
- touchdown;
- ground roll;
- total landing distance to full stop;
- comparison with POH reference performance.

Manual flight-card observations and digital Flight Test Instrumentation data were cross-checked during post-processing.

---

## Flight-Test Planning

Before flight execution, a complete **Flight Test Planning Document** was prepared.

The planning phase included:

- mission objectives;
- aircraft configuration;
- aircraft operating limitations;
- test-airspace constraints;
- airport procedures;
- expected atmospheric conditions;
- test sequencing;
- instrumentation selection;
- required measurements;
- data-acquisition procedures;
- correction methods;
- detailed test checklists;
- safety and hazard assessment.

The full mission sequence included aircraft familiarisation, pre-flight briefing, test instrumentation checks, take-off testing, phugoid manoeuvres, wind determination, stall testing, landing testing and post-flight procedures.

---

## Instrumentation and Data Acquisition

The campaign combined native aircraft instrumentation with dedicated flight-test equipment.

Measured or reconstructed quantities included:

- airspeed;
- altitude;
- attitude;
- acceleration;
- GPS position and ground speed;
- engine / propulsion parameters;
- atmospheric conditions;
- control-surface information;
- event timing.

Additional instrumentation included Flight Test Instrumentation systems, GPS/RTK-based measurements and cockpit recording systems.

Redundancy between digital acquisition and manual flight cards was intentionally maintained to support cross-validation and reduce the risk of losing critical test information.

---

## Safety and Risk Management

Safety planning formed an integral part of the campaign.

A dedicated **Test Hazard Analysis (THA)** was developed for:

- take-off testing;
- phugoid testing;
- stall testing;
- landing testing.

Hazards were classified using severity and probability categories, and mitigation measures were defined before flight execution.

Operational limits from the aircraft POHs, airport procedures, weather restrictions and airspace limitations were incorporated directly into the test plan.

---

## Data Reduction and Analysis

Post-flight analysis was performed using **MATLAB and Dewesoft**, combining Flight Test Instrumentation data with manually recorded flight-card information.

The analysis included:

- atmospheric corrections;
- wind corrections;
- aircraft mass corrections;
- data consistency checks;
- comparison between independent measurement sources;
- interpolation and regression;
- comparison with POH performance;
- interpretation of test scatter and uncertainty.

Special attention was given to separating genuine aircraft behaviour from effects caused by turbulence, aircraft loading, pilot technique and instrumentation uncertainty.

---

## Selected Results

### Take-off Performance

After correction for test conditions, the measured take-off performance was in close agreement with manufacturer reference data.

- **SW121:** corrected total take-off performance was approximately **4% different from POH**.
- **SW128:** corrected performance was within approximately **1% of POH**.

The comparison between FTI and manual flight-card data also showed good consistency.

---

### Stall Behaviour

The SW121 produced six acceptable stall test points and showed generally benign behaviour.

The SW128 produced several valid points, but one test point exhibited a **roll-break behaviour** and was rejected as certification evidence.

The finding led to a recommendation for targeted re-testing under improved atmospheric conditions and over a wider CG range.

---

### Landing Performance

Landing distances were successfully reconstructed for both aircraft.

The SW121 showed close agreement with reference performance, while the SW128 showed a larger discrepancy in ground roll.

The analysis identified turbulence, conservative pilot technique and runway conditions as the main contributors to the difference.

---

### Model Cross-Validation

A numerical FLOW5 model was used as an additional comparison for longitudinal stability.

The model reproduced the qualitative characteristics of the stick-fixed phugoid response, while quantitative differences highlighted the influence of simplified inertia modelling, omitted propeller effects and atmospheric turbulence.

This provided a useful example of how numerical modelling can support flight testing while still requiring experimental validation.

---

## Test Findings and Engineering Judgement

One of the most important aspects of the project was not simply obtaining numerical results, but evaluating their validity.

Examples included:

- rejecting a stall test point due to unacceptable roll-break characteristics;
- assessing turbulence as a source of repeatability error;
- identifying CG limitations that could not be fully corrected in post-processing;
- separating instrumentation uncertainty from aircraft-performance deviations;
- recommending targeted re-testing where the available evidence was insufficient.

This reinforced the importance of engineering judgement in addition to data acquisition and numerical processing.

---

## Recommendations

The final report proposed improvements for future campaigns, including:

- improved real-time wind measurement;
- testing at more representative mass and CG conditions;
- additional repeated test runs for statistical confidence;
- tighter standardisation of pilot technique;
- dedicated investigation of SW128 roll-break stall behaviour;
- improved event-marker automation;
- expanded phugoid testing across the operational envelope;
- improved instrumentation for environmental effects.

